{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '175,385p'\"","aggregated_output":"   175\t\n   176\t---\n   177\t\n   178\t## 3. The expander (`yin.vm.macro`)\n   179\t\n   180\t### 3.1 Contract\n   181\t\n   182\t```\n   183\texpand-batch : datoms ctx → {:status :ok     :datoms out :root-eid r :log [...] :ctx ctx'}\n   184\t                          | {:status :error  :error {:kind ..} :log [...] :ctx ctx'}\n   185\texpand       : datoms ctx → out | throws                ; convenience over expand-batch\n   186\tdefinitions  : datoms → {sym lambda-ast|nil}           ; syntactic harvest, last wins; nil = removal\n   187\tdrain-errors : expander → [expander' errors]           ; §5\n   188\tinvoke       : lambda-ast operand-asts ctx → value      ; one body, §3.3\n   189\tvalid-ast?   : value opts → nil | {:kind :invalid-output :at path}\n   190\tmark-tail    : ast → ast                               ; §3.4, whole-tree recompute\n   191\tstep         : expander → expander'                    ; one forwarder step, §5\n   192\tevent-schema                                           ; §4.1\n   193\tstdlib-forms, prelude\n   194\t```\n   195\t\n   196\t`ctx` is the expander's process state and is threaded through every call:\n   197\t\n   198\t```\n   199\t{:store    {sym lambda-ast}       ; macro environment, persisted across batches\n   200\t :alloc    {:next-eid n}          ; monotonic negative counter, never re-seeded upward\n   201\t :guards   {:max-depth 100  :max-nodes-per-expansion 10000\n   202\t            :max-steps 100000  :max-datoms-per-batch nil}\n   203\t :eval     (fn [lambda-ast env prelude budget] → value)   ; §3.3\n   204\t :t        0}                     ; batch counter; incremented once per expand-batch\n   205\t```\n   206\t\n   207\t`expand-batch`:\n   208\t\n   209\t1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms` and checked as a\n   210\t   whole — **every entity in the index, not only those reachable from the\n   211\t   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every\n   212\t   `:yin/type` is in the closed vocabulary (§3.3), every ref resolves to an\n   213\t   entity in the index, the whole graph is acyclic, and every ground value is\n   214\t   plain data — all *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). An\n   215\t   in-memory medium can carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input\n   216\t   guarantee holds only if nothing else is admitted. A failure here is\n   217\t   `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input :reason :unknown-type |\n   218\t   :dangling-ref | :cyclic | :host-value :eid e}}` carrying a **batch-level\n   219\t   event** (§4.1) — allocated from the watermark alone, since the index may\n   220\t   be unusable — and the advanced `:ctx`. Diagnostics name entities, never\n   221\t   carry the offending values.\n   222\t2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the\n   223\t   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →\n   224\t   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a\n   225\t   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of\n   226\t   that name. Harvest is *syntactic* and whole-batch by policy: a definition\n   227\t   in an unexecuted branch, or after its first use in the same batch, is\n   228\t   still in force for the whole batch. (Clojure defines a macro only when the\n   229\t   `defmacro` form executes; this design does not, and says so.)\n   230\t3. **Replace.** Each macro definition node is replaced by\n   231\t   `{:type :literal :value <sym>}`, so a batch whose root is a definition\n   232\t   still forwards a complete program and a REPL prints the name. Plain\n   233\t   `yin/def`s are forwarded untouched.\n   234\t4. **Expand.** `expand-node` (§3.2) runs from the root over the index.\n   235\t5. **Post-harvest.** The *final* tree is scanned for `(yin/def <literal sym>\n   236\t   v)` with `v` not a macro lambda, and each such `sym` is `dissoc`ed from\n   237\t   `:store` — this catches redefinitions that only take the `yin/def` shape\n   238\t   after expansion, `(defn m …)` above all. **Ordering rule:** step 2's store\n   239\t   governs the whole current batch; step 5's removals take effect from the\n   240\t   next batch. Within one batch a macro `m` and a `(defn m …)` therefore both\n   241\t   apply to their own forms in source order as Clojure would not, and the\n   242\t   design accepts that in exchange for a one-pass, deterministic rule.\n   243\t6. **Mark tail.** `mark-tail` recomputes `:tail?` over the whole final tree\n   244\t   (§3.4).\n   245\t7. **Emit.** Output is emitted bottom-up as a self-contained batch with a\n   246\t   fresh `:yin/root` fact; the log copy carries `m` per §4.1.\n   247\t8. **Scan.** A final walk asserts no lambda with `:yin/macro? true` remains.\n   248\t   Fixpoint for applications is by construction (§3.2); this scan is the\n   249\t   guard for decision 5.\n   250\t\n   251\tAn `:error` result carries the event (§4.1) in `:log` and the advanced\n   252\t`:ctx`; `:datoms` is absent. `expand` unwraps `:ok` and throws on `:error`\n   253\tfor callers that want the exception. Same inputs, same output, on every\n   254\thost.\n   255\t\n   256\t**Working representation.** Traversal, cycle detection, and shadow tracking\n   257\trun over *eids in the index*. Plain AST maps are materialised only at the\n   258\tboundary of one body invocation — operands decoded for `invoke`, the returned\n   259\tvalue validated and re-encoded to datoms with fresh ids, and re-expansion\n   260\tcontinues over the index of the batch-so-far. Internal identity never\n   261\tappears in the map representation macros see (`valid-ast?` rejects `:eid`).\n   262\t\n   263\t**Allocation.** `:alloc` is seeded per batch to\n   264\t`(min (:next-eid alloc) (dec min-eid-in-batch) (- (inc datom/first-user-id)))`\n   265\t— the incoming watermark is a term, so the counter only ever decreases across\n   266\ta session and event eids on the accumulated log never collide. It is returned\n   267\tadvanced in both `:ok` and `:error` results; a retry of a staged flush reuses\n   268\tthe staged allocation and never allocates again.\n   269\t\n   270\t### 3.2 The algorithm\n   271\t\n   272\tTraversal visits ref attributes in a **fixed order** so fresh ids and\n   273\tgensyms are deterministic across hosts: `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`\n   274\t(left to right), `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`,\n   275\t`:yin/body`, `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, `:yin/source`. `shadow` is the\n   276\tset of names bound by enclosing `:lambda :params`.\n   277\t\n   278\t```\n   279\tmacro-of ctx eid shadow :\n   280\t  case operator(eid)\n   281\t    :variable v  → v ∉ shadow ∧ (:store ctx) v\n   282\t    :lambda  l   → l when (:macro? l)\n   283\t    _            → nil\n   284\t\n   285\texpand-node ctx eid shadow depth :\n   286\t  if type(eid) = :application ∧ (m = macro-of ctx eid shadow):\n   287\t      ev        = fresh-event ctx eid m                  ; allocated first: every failure below is recorded on ev\n   288\t      if depth ≥ :max-depth: fail ev {:kind :depth-guard :depth :chain}\n   289\t      operands  = [decode c | c ∈ operands(eid)]         ; unexpanded AST maps\n   290\t      out       = invoke m operands ctx                  ; §3.3 — arity, fuel, suspension fail on ev\n   291\t      check valid-ast? out {:allow-macro-lambda? false}  ; node count ≤ :max-nodes-per-expansion\n   292\t      root'     = encode out (fresh ids from :alloc)     ; joins the index of the batch-so-far\n   293\t      record ev :yin/expansion-root root'\n   294\t      return expand-node ctx root' shadow (depth+1)      ; re-expand the output under the call site's scope\n   295\t  else if type(eid) = :application:\n   296\t      op'       = expand-node ctx operator(eid) shadow depth        ; operator FIRST\n   297\t      eid'      = if op' = operator(eid) then eid else copy eid with operator op'\n   298\t      if macro-of ctx eid' shadow:\n   299\t          return expand-node ctx eid' shadow depth        ; became a macro call: takes the first arm with the ORIGINAL operands\n   300\t      operands' = [expand-node ctx c shadow depth | c ∈ operands(eid)]\n   301\t      return if operands' = operands(eid) then eid' else copy eid' with operands'\n   302\t  else:\n   303\t      shadow'   = if type(eid) = :lambda then shadow ∪ params(eid) else shadow\n   304\t      children' = [expand-node ctx c shadow' depth | c ∈ children(eid) in fixed order]\n   305\t      return if children' = children(eid) then eid else copy eid with children'\n   306\t```\n   307\t\n   308\t`fail ev …` writes `:yin/error` on the event and unwinds to `expand-batch`,\n   309\twhich returns the `:error` result with that event in `:log`. Cycle and\n   310\tdangling-ref detection happened at admission (§3.1 step 1), so this walk is\n   311\tover a known-acyclic graph; output encoded from validated plain maps is\n   312\tacyclic by construction.\n   313\t\n   314\tThe application branch expands the **operator before the operands** so that\n   315\tan operator which rewrites into a macro name hands that macro its operands\n   316\t*as written*: in `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with `choose` → `defn`, `defn`\n   317\tmust receive `(x 1)` unexpanded so the lambda it builds can shadow `x`.\n   318\tExpanding operands first would violate outermost-first for exactly the\n   319\tmacros (binders) that depend on it. Recognition is not an expansion — the\n   320\tre-entry is at the same depth — and a successful re-check goes straight into\n   321\tthe invocation arm, which re-expands at `depth+1`, so a chain of generated\n   322\tapplications consumes depth and cannot rewrite forever at constant depth.\n   323\t\n   324\tProperties, each a Phase 1 test:\n   325\t\n   326\t- **Outermost first.** A macro sees its operands as written. This is what\n   327\t  makes `->`-style macros expressible and what retires the shadow hints:\n   328\t  in `(defn f [x] (x 1))`, `defn` expands *before* its operands are examined,\n   329\t  yielding `(yin/def f (lambda [x] (x 1)))`; re-expansion then meets `(x 1)`\n   330\t  under `:lambda [x]`, so `x` is shadowed and left alone even if a macro\n   331\t  named `x` exists. yang's frontend hint existed only because the frontend\n   332\t  had to decide this before expansion.\n   333\t- **Fixpoint by construction.** Every node reachable from the final root\n   334\t  was visited after its last rewrite, *including* an application whose\n   335\t  operator was rewritten into a macro name by a child expansion\n   336\t  (`((choose) x)` where `choose` expands to `m`): the application branch\n   337\t  re-checks after expanding the operator and before touching the operands.\n   338\t- **Depth** counts nesting of expansion inside expansion output. Direct\n   339\t  recursion without a base case fails at 100 with\n   340\t  `{:kind :depth-guard :depth 100 :chain [eid ...]}`. Siblings do not\n   341\t  accumulate depth.\n   342\t- **Shadowing is lexical and complete.** `let`, `do`, and every binder every\n   343\t  frontend emits lower to `:lambda :params`; `yin/def` is global and is the\n   344\t  store. There is no other binder in the Universal AST. The shadow set is\n   345\t  extended *at the lambda node*, so an operator-position lambda's params\n   346\t  shadow its body and not its sibling operands.\n   347\t- **Generated macro lambdas are rejected in v1 — wherever they appear.** A\n   348\t  macro whose output contains a `:macro? true` lambda, as a definition value\n   349\t  *or* inline in operator position, fails `{:kind :invalid-output :reason\n   350\t  :generated-macro}`; `valid-ast?` is called with `:allow-macro-lambda?\n   351\t  false` on expansion output. `macro-of`'s inline-lambda case therefore only\n   352\t  ever fires on lambdas the *source* batch contained. Macro-defining macros\n   353\t  and generated inline macros are reserved together (§7); admitting the\n   354\t  second without the first would need a rule for keeping a generated macro\n   355\t  body as transformer syntax through re-expansion, which is the same\n   356\t  problem.\n   357\t- **Failure leaves a record.** The attempt event is allocated before the\n   358\t  depth guard, resolution, and invocation, so every failure after that\n   359\t  point is written with `:yin/error` (§4.1) and reaches the log through the\n   360\t  `:error` result (§5).\n   361\t\n   362\t### 3.3 Executing the macro lambda\n   363\t\n   364\t`invoke` binds positional params one AST map each and `& rest` to a vector\n   365\tof the remaining maps (arity mismatch is\n   366\t`{:kind :arity :macro eid :expected n :got m}`) and calls\n   367\t`((:eval ctx) lambda-ast env prelude budget)`.\n   368\t\n   369\tThe expander supplies its own body runner; today it is a throwaway\n   370\t`yin.vm.ast-walker` driven by `step` in a counted loop, because\n   371\t`engine/run-loop` has no fuel and a body that loops forever would otherwise\n   372\tstall the forwarder:\n   373\t\n   374\t```\n   375\t(defn run-bounded [lambda-ast env prelude budget]\n   376\t  (let [vm (ast-walker/create-vm {:env env :primitives (merge vm/primitives prelude)})\n   377\t        vm (ast-walker/vm-load-program vm (vm/ast->datoms (:body lambda-ast)))]\n   378\t    (loop [vm vm, n 0]\n   379\t      (cond (> n budget)            (throw (ex-info \"Macro body exceeded step budget\" {:kind :fuel-guard :steps n}))\n   380\t            (vm/blocked? vm)        (throw (ex-info \"Macro body suspended\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   381\t            (seq (:parked vm))      (throw (ex-info \"Macro body parked\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   382\t            (seq (:ready-queue vm)) (throw (ex-info \"Macro body scheduled work\" {:kind :suspended}))\n   383\t            (vm/halted? vm)         (vm/value vm)\n   384\t            :else                   (recur (vm/step vm) (inc n))))))\n   385\t```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
