{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '520,785p'\"","aggregated_output":"   520\t\n   521\t---\n   522\t\n   523\t## 4. Provenance\n   524\t\n   525\t### 4.1 The expansion event\n   526\t\n   527\tEvery attempt, successful or not, produces one event; so does every batch\n   528\trejected at admission. Events and the `m`-tagged output are appended to the\n   529\t**log** medium, when the expander has one; `program-out` receives only\n   530\tprogram.\n   531\t\n   532\t```\n   533\t[ev :yin/type           :macro-expand-event  t default-op]\n   534\t[ev :yin/source-batch   <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; which source batch; always present\n   535\t[ev :yin/source-call    <call-eid>           t default-op]   ; eid in that batch, or in the log copy of the enclosing expansion; absent on an admission failure\n   536\t[ev :yin/macro-name     <sym>                t default-op]   ; when the operator was a :variable; absent for an inline macro lambda or an admission failure\n   537\t[ev :yin/macro          <lambda-eid>         t default-op]   ; only when durable (§4.2)\n   538\t[ev :yin/expansion-root <root'>              t default-op]   ; on success; names the LOG copy\n   539\t[ev :yin/error          {:kind ...}          t default-op]   ; on failure; plain data naming entities, never carrying input values\n   540\t[ev :yin/timestamp      <ctx :t>             t default-op]   ; logical, never a host clock\n   541\t```\n   542\t\n   543\tAn **admission-failure event** is the same entity with only `:yin/type`,\n   544\t`:yin/source-batch`, `:yin/error`, and `:yin/timestamp`: the batch had no\n   545\tusable call or macro to name. It is allocated from `:alloc`'s watermark\n   546\t(which needs no index), so a corrupt batch still produces exactly one\n   547\tplain-data event, advances `:ctx` once, and is retried through the same\n   548\tper-medium staging as any other log payload — never regenerated.\n   549\t\n   550\tThe log copy of expansion output carries `m = ev`; log copies of unchanged\n   551\toperands and ancestors carry `m = default-op` (re-parenting, not macro\n   552\toutput). **`program-out` datoms all carry `default-op`**: `m = ev` would be\n   553\ta cross-medium ref (§4.2). A nested expansion's `:yin/source-call` names a\n   554\tnode in the log copy of the enclosing expansion's output, which is how the\n   555\tchain source → event → root → event → root is walked for generated syntax.\n   556\t\"Every datom this expansion produced\" is `[?d _ _ _ ?ev]` on the log.\n   557\t\n   558\t`yin.vm.macro/event-schema` declares these attributes (`:yin/source-call`,\n   559\t`:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root` as refs) for compositions that commit a\n   560\tlog to `dao.space`; the transactor relocates only declared refs, so a\n   561\tcomposition merges this fragment with `yin.vm/schema` before committing.\n   562\t\n   563\t### 4.2 Identity across media\n   564\t\n   565\t`program-in`, `program-out`, and the log are different media. A ref written\n   566\ton one that names an entity on another is meaningful only when the named\n   567\tid is **durable** — a permanent positive id from a committed stream. The\n   568\ttransactor (`dao.space.transact`) resolves tempids within one transaction;\n   569\tcommitting two batches separately does not connect them. Therefore:\n   570\t\n   571\t- `program-out` batches are self-contained: unchanged subtrees are copied,\n   572\t  with fresh ids, not referenced (decision 8).\n   573\t- `:yin/source-call` on an event names the call's eid *as it appeared in\n   574\t  the source batch*, qualified by `:yin/source-batch`; it is descriptive\n   575\t  until both media are committed under one resolution. `:yin/macro` is\n   576\t  written only when the macro's id is durable; otherwise `:yin/macro-name`\n   577\t  carries the name. Names are metadata, never identity repair.\n   578\t- `:yin/expansion-root` names the **log copy** of the output. The executed\n   579\t  program on `program-out` is a distinct copy with distinct ids; this design\n   580\t  promises no id-level correspondence between them. A composition that needs\n   581\t  one commits program and log in one transaction (they are one batch to the\n   582\t  transactor) or records the mapping itself.\n   583\t- A pipeline that wants queryable provenance commits `program-in` first and\n   584\t  expands against durable ids — the ordering rule of\n   585\t  `cross-language-macro.md` §4, retained. `ADR 0003` makes `dao.space` the\n   586\t  medium for that commit.\n   587\t\n   588\t### 4.3 Authority\n   589\t\n   590\tWrite authority on a medium is the whole model. Defining a macro is\n   591\tappending a definition to `program-in`; running a program is appending to\n   592\t`program-out`; both are decided by whoever holds the writer, and\n   593\t`dao.stream.md` puts that decision in the composition. A Shibi\n   594\tcapability, when it exists, is verified at the medium's boundary before the\n   595\tappend, not inside any VM. The old `:macro-authorize` VM option is gone\n   596\tbecause there is nothing left inside a VM to authorize.\n   597\t\n   598\t---\n   599\t\n   600\t## 5. The expander as an observer\n   601\t\n   602\tThe expander is driven by `yin.vm.stream-observer/run-on-stream` with\n   603\tits state in the `:vm` slot — the coordination inspects no evaluator field,\n   604\tso it drives an expander as readily as a VM:\n   605\t\n   606\t```\n   607\texpander = {:ctx ctx\n   608\t            :out writer        :out-staged  nil|datoms\n   609\t            :log writer|nil    :log-staged  nil|datoms\n   610\t            :forwarded 0       ; batches appended ok to :out since the last drain\n   611\t            :errors []}        ; expansion failures since the last drain\n   612\t\n   613\tready?       (fn [x] (and (nil? (:out-staged x)) (nil? (:log-staged x))))\n   614\tload-program (fn [x batch]\n   615\t               (let [r (expand-batch batch (:ctx x))]\n   616\t                 (-> x (assoc :ctx (:ctx r))\n   617\t                       (assoc :out-staged (when (= :ok (:status r)) (:datoms r)))\n   618\t                       (assoc :log-staged (when (:log x) (:log r)))\n   619\t                       (cond-> (= :error (:status r)) (update :errors conj (:error r))))))\n   620\trun-vm       flush                    ; below\n   621\tdrain-errors (fn [x] [(assoc x :errors [] :forwarded 0) {:errors (:errors x) :forwarded (:forwarded x)}])\n   622\t```\n   623\t\n   624\t`:errors` and `:forwarded` are **read-and-reset** by the driver through\n   625\t`drain-errors`, once per driver round, after `run-on-stream` returns and\n   626\tbefore the driver decides what to do next. Nothing else clears them; a\n   627\tdriver that never drains sees an ever-growing list, which is its bug, not a\n   628\tstuck expander (the cursor has moved on regardless).\n   629\t\n   630\t**Flush** delivers each staged payload to its own medium and clears *that*\n   631\tslot on `ok`, independently:\n   632\t\n   633\t| Outcome on a medium | Effect on its slot |\n   634\t|---|---|\n   635\t| `ok` | slot ← nil; for `:out`, `:forwarded` += 1 |\n   636\t| `full` | slot kept; expander stays not-ready; the same payload is retried next round. `full` may be permanent; retention is correct, progress is the composition's to arrange |\n   637\t| `closed`, `invalid-value`, `transport-error` | legitimate protocol outcomes that this forwarder cannot continue from: throw, preserving the original outcome and both slots' state in the thrown value, so the composition decides |\n   638\t\n   639\tOnce a medium has answered `ok` its payload is never appended again by an\n   640\tordinary retry, so `out = ok, log = full` re-tries only the log. A batch\n   641\tthat expands with `:error` stages nothing for `program-out` and stages the\n   642\tevent for the log; the source cursor advances (the load returned), the\n   643\tfailure is on the log, and the driver drains it. Program and log are\n   644\tindependent media: this design offers *at-most-once per medium* and batch\n   645\torder per medium under ordinary retries, not atomic visibility across the\n   646\ttwo and not crash recovery; a composition that needs atomicity commits both\n   647\tto one `dao.space` transaction (§4.2).\n   648\t\n   649\t`run-on-stream`'s contract supplies the rest: a `full` leaves the expander\n   650\tnot-ready with the exact payload staged, so the next round retries the\n   651\tappend before observing again; `gap` on `program-in` is counted by the\n   652\tobserver and is the composition's policy, as for any observer. What remains\n   653\ta *throw* out of `load-program` is a forwarder defect (codec failure, index\n   654\tfailure); the cursor does not advance and the batch is re-read, which is\n   655\tthe correct behaviour for a bug and the wrong one for bad input — hence\n   656\tdecision 11.\n   657\t\n   658\t**Prerequisite on the observer (Phase 0).** `run-on-stream` today publishes\n   659\tits successor session only on return. If it forwards A, then B's load or run\n   660\tthrows, the caller still holds the pre-A session and a retry forwards A again\n   661\t(reproduced by the round-2 review against the current observer). This is a\n   662\tdefect of the generic coordination, not of this design — a VM loader that\n   663\tthrows on B after loading A has the same problem — and it is fixed there.\n   664\tThe fix **keeps the throw** and carries the partial session in the\n   665\texception's data: `(ex-info … {:session {:observer o' :vm vm'} …})`. A\n   666\treturn-shaped `{:observer :vm :error}` was considered and rejected: every\n   667\texisting caller reasons \"a throw means the round failed\", and a value-shaped\n   668\terror would let an un-updated caller continue silently past a defect. With\n   669\tthe throw kept, recovery is opt-in: a caller that catches resumes from\n   670\t`:session`; one that does not keeps today's semantics. The carried session\n   671\tmust distinguish *where* the failure was: a **load** failure carries the\n   672\tcursor before the failing batch (it will be re-read); a **run/flush**\n   673\tfailure after a successful load carries the cursor *after* that batch and\n   674\tthe `:vm` as the failing `run-vm` left it (for the expander: its partially\n   675\tflushed slots), so a retry neither re-reads B nor re-appends what B already\n   676\tdelivered. This document depends on that fix and does not work around it.\n   677\t\n   678\t```\n   679\t  yang ──▶ program-in ──observe──▶ expander ──append──▶ program-out ──observe──▶ evaluator\n   680\t                                       │\n   681\t                                       └──append──▶ log (events, m-tagged output)\n   682\t```\n   683\t\n   684\tEvery evaluator observes `program-out` through its unchanged loader:\n   685\t`ast-walker/vm-load-program`, `linearize/loader ∘ semantic`, the stack\n   686\tcompiler. None composes anything macro-related.\n   687\t\n   688\t---\n   689\t\n   690\t## 6. Compositions\n   691\t\n   692\t### 6.1 `yin.repl`\n   693\t\n   694\t`make-session` builds `program-in`, the expander (with `stdlib-forms`\n   695\tpre-loaded into its store), `program-out`, an optional log medium, and the\n   696\tevaluator's observer on `program-out`. An evaluation round is: append the\n   697\tfrontend's datoms to `program-in`; drive the expander with `run-on-stream`\n   698\tuntil ready; `drain-errors`; **if `:forwarded` > 0**, drive the evaluator\n   699\twith `run-on-stream` on `program-out` and drain output; **if `:errors` is\n   700\tnon-empty**, print them. Both can hold in one round when the expander\n   701\tconsumed more than one source batch (A forwarded, B failed): A runs once, B\n   702\tis reported once. The shell appends exactly one batch per input, so in the\n   703\tordinary case one input yields one result, and correlation needs no further\n   704\tmachinery. `eval-ast` takes this same path (`vm/eval` is no longer a program\n   705\tpath in the shell). `(compile expr)`\n   706\trenders AST, source datoms, expanded datoms, and events. `(reset)` and\n   707\t`(vm ...)` rebuild the whole session, which clears the expander's store with\n   708\tthe evaluator's. `repl-state` lists the macro names the expander's store\n   709\tholds, so an \"unable to resolve `defn`\" is diagnosable at a glance.\n   710\t\n   711\tThe shell's frontends are untouched: `yang.clojure/compile` and\n   712\t`compile-program` lose their `macro-env` arities; `yang.python` and\n   713\t`yang.php` never had any and gain none.\n   714\t\n   715\t### 6.2 `yang.clojure`\n   716\t\n   717\t`defmacro` stays a special form lowering to `(yin/def name <lambda macro?\n   718\ttrue>)`. Deleted: `initial-macro-env`, `shadow-macro-env`,\n   719\t`compile-macro-operands`, `compile-defn`'s native fallback (the `defn` macro\n   720\tis always in the expander's store), the `macro-env` parameter on every\n   721\t`compile-*`, and `:yin/macro-expand` emission. `compile-program` returns\n   722\tAST only.\n   723\t\n   724\t### 6.3 Non-homoiconic frontends\n   725\t\n   726\t`yang.python` and `yang.php` emit `:application` for every call, as they do\n   727\ttoday. A call to a macro defined in Clojure is expanded because the\n   728\texpander's store has it — cross-language macro use costs the frontend\n   729\tnothing. Authoring macros from Python or PHP is the prelude behind a\n   730\t`defmacro` surface and is reserved; the Python tokenizer has no list-literal\n   731\tproduction, so a Python `defn` call is not yet expressible.\n   732\t\n   733\t### 6.4 Build pipelines\n   734\t\n   735\tA build composes the same forwarder between file-backed media\n   736\t(`dao.stream.file.md`), committing `program-in` to `dao.space` first when it\n   737\twants durable provenance (§4.2). The expander's store can be seeded from a\n   738\tcommitted stream with `definitions`; a DaoDB query for\n   739\t`[?e :yin/macro? true]` joined to its `yin/def` is a future producer of the\n   740\tsame map.\n   741\t\n   742\t### 6.5 Cross-platform\n   743\t\n   744\tOne `.cljc`; the expander is maps, vectors, and `reduce`. Symbols from\n   745\t`yin/gensym-sym` are built with `symbol`. The `#?(:cljd …)`-first\n   746\tconditional and `#?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default)` catch form\n   747\tare used as elsewhere in v2. Test discovery: confirm \"Testing\n   748\tyin.vm.macro-test\" in the shadow `:node-test` output.\n   749\t\n   750\t---\n   751\t\n   752\t## 7. Phased implementation roadmap\n   753\t\n   754\t### Phase 0 — Spec and codec\n   755\t\n   756\t- This document. `macro-design.md` and `cross-language-macro.md` amended to\n   757\t  point here. The divergence register's macro item becomes: \"user-defined\n   758\t  macros are expanded by a process between media, never by an evaluator;\n   759\t  call sites are ordinary applications; `eval` does not expand.\" Its Macros\n   760\t  section records the deviations of Appendix A.\n   761\t- `yin.vm`: `schema` gains `:yin/root` and `:yin/macro-name`, drops\n   762\t  `:yin/phase-policy`, `:yin/phase`, `:yin/capability`, `:yin/source-call`,\n   763\t  `:yin/macro`, `:yin/expansion-root`, `:yin/error` (event attributes move\n   764\t  to the expander's emitter). `ast->datoms-with-root` emits the root fact;\n   765\t  `index-datoms` honours it (last wins, dangling error); the codec drops the\n   766\t  `:yin/macro-expand` arms and the `:phase-policy` handling.\n   767\t- `ast-walker`: the `:lambda` arm and `datoms->ast` continue to ignore\n   768\t  `:macro?`; nothing else changes. The namespace docstring's \"no macro\n   769\t  branch\" remark becomes the statement of decision 1.\n   770\t- `stream-observer/run-on-stream`: throw with the partial session in\n   771\t  `ex-data` (§5 prerequisite); existing callers are unchanged. Tests: A\n   772\t  forwarded, B's load throws — the carried cursor is before B and a retry\n   773\t  from it leaves exactly one A on the destination; A forwarded, B's `run-vm`\n   774\t  throws — the carried cursor is after B and `:vm` is as `run-vm` left it; a\n   775\t  terminal read after a successful batch carries the post-batch session.\n   776\t- New `test/yin/vm_test.cljc` for the codec: root fact wins; dangling\n   777\t  root errors; heuristic fallback unchanged; `compile` output unchanged\n   778\t  *modulo the root fact* for macro-free programs (the fact is unconditional).\n   779\t\n   780\t### Phase 1 — Expander and yang\n   781\t\n   782\tDeliverables: `src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc` (`expand-batch`, `expand`,\n   783\t`definitions`, `invoke`, `bind-params`, `valid-ast?`, `mark-tail`,\n   784\t`run-bounded`, prelude, `stdlib-forms`, `step`); `yang.clojure` per §6.2.\n   785\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
