{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.macro.md | sed -n '785,1010p'\"","aggregated_output":"   785\t\n   786\tTests (`macro_test.cljc`): `defn` via `stdlib-forms` equals the previously\n   787\tnative lowering; definition consumed and replaced by a name literal; a\n   788\tdefinition in an unexecuted branch is harvested; last definition wins;\n   789\t**redefinition as a plain function removes the macro and the next call is\n   790\tforwarded as an application — via a source `yin/def`, via `(defn m …)`\n   791\t(post-harvest, effective next batch), and via `(def m other-fn)`**;\n   792\toutermost-first (`->`-style macro); shadowing by `let`, `fn`, and by a\n   793\t`defn`'s own params without any frontend hint; `(fn [m] (m 1))` with `m` a\n   794\tmacro is left intact; a macro name in operand position is left as a\n   795\tvariable; **an operator macro returning a macro name is expanded with its\n   796\toperands unexpanded (`((choose) f [x] (x 1))` with a macro `x` in scope\n   797\tyields a lambda whose body still says `(x 1)`), and an operator-selected\n   798\tmacro that discards an operand containing a failing macro call succeeds**;\n   799\ta macro emitting a macro lambda — as a definition or inline — fails\n   800\t`:generated-macro`; depth guard with chain; per-expansion bound from node\n   801\tcount before emission; per-batch bound; step budget on an infinite body;\n   802\tparked body rejected; **cyclic operand, dangling ref, unknown node type, a\n   803\thost value in a literal, and a cycle inside a disconnected definition all\n   804\tfail at admission before any decode, each producing one admission event\n   805\tthat survives a log `full`**; invalid output including a host function inside a\n   806\tliteral and an `:eid` key; arity and `&`; **gensyms differ across two\n   807\tbatches with overlapping tempids when ctx is threaded, and are equal on\n   808\treplay of the same ctx**; a stream effect in a body fails with the\n   809\tconstructor error; `mark-tail` on `if`/`let`/lambda spines **and clears a\n   810\tstale flag on marked syntax moved into an operand and into an `if` test**;\n   811\ta macro-free deeply nested batch passes; determinism (two runs, equal\n   812\tvectors); macro-free batch returned equal modulo the root fact; a\n   813\tcorpus-scale batch at 80% of a configured per-batch bound passes; the\n   814\tforwarder driven through `observer/run-on-stream`: `out=ok, log=full`\n   815\tretries only the log; `out=full` retries only the program; repeated `full`;\n   816\tno log medium; **an expansion failure advances the cursor, stages only the\n   817\tevent, and appears in `drain-errors`; a failed batch then a successful one\n   818\tin a single `run-on-stream` call yields `:forwarded 1` and one error; a\n   819\tdrained expander reports nothing on the next drain**; a forwarder-defect\n   820\tthrow does not advance the cursor.\n   821\t\n   822\t### Phase 2 — REPL composition\n   823\t\n   824\tDeliverables per §6.1. Tests: define in one input, use in the next; use\n   825\tfrom `:python` after defining in `:clojure`; `(reset)` forgets; `(compile)`\n   826\trenders four stages; a runtime error from a macro-named variable in operand\n   827\tposition is an unbound-variable error, not a call; a bad macro input prints\n   828\tits error and the *next* input evaluates normally (no head-of-line block);\n   829\t`repl-state` lists the store's macro names.\n   830\t\n   831\tDeferred to the semantic VM's own Phases 1–2 (`yin.vm.semantic` and\n   832\t`linearize` do not exist yet): the `:semantic` VM type runs the same session\n   833\twith equal values, and a tail-position macro in a 10⁵-iteration loop keeps\n   834\tcontinuation depth 0 — measured by inspecting `k`, since equal values alone\n   835\tdo not prove it.\n   836\t\n   837\t### Phase 3 — Cross-language verification\n   838\t\n   839\tTests (`python_macro_test.clj`, `php_macro_test.clj`): a scalar-signature\n   840\tClojure macro `(defmacro twice [f] (yin/application f [(yin/application f\n   841\t[(yin/literal 1)])]))` called as `twice(inc_fn)` from Python and\n   842\t`twice($inc_fn)` from PHP; `yin/name-of` with a Python string, a Clojure\n   843\tsymbol, and a `:variable`; a PHP `for`-loop variable shadowing a macro name;\n   844\tthe source batch unchanged after expansion; equal values on walker and\n   845\tsemantic. Python `defn` stays reserved until the parser has list literals.\n   846\t\n   847\t### Reserved\n   848\t\n   849\tThe expander as a `yin.vm` program (requires a bounded-child-evaluation\n   850\tprimitive — the general form of `run-bounded`); macro-defining macros\n   851\t(generated definitions, with a precedence rule for definitions introduced\n   852\tmid-batch); macro helpers via expansion of macro bodies before storing;\n   853\tpayload-size bounds at admission for untrusted authors; Shibi at the medium\n   854\tboundary; automatic hygiene; DaoDB-backed store seeding; durable\n   855\tcross-medium provenance and program↔log correspondence as a transactor\n   856\tconcern; expansion memo keyed by call structure (sound because expansion is\n   857\tpure, but each expansion mints its own event and gensyms); Python/PHP\n   858\t`defmacro` surfaces.\n   859\t\n   860\t---\n   861\t\n   862\t## Appendix A. Deviations from prior documents, with reasons\n   863\t\n   864\tFrom `macro-design.md` (v1):\n   865\t\n   866\t1. **AST maps, not eids, as macro arguments.** Every frontend speaks maps;\n   867\t   the datom form is the transport, not the API.\n   868\t2. **Outermost-first, not post-order.** `->`-style macros are impossible\n   869\t   innermost-first, and outermost-first is what makes lexical shadowing\n   870\t   computable without frontend hints.\n   871\t3. **Fixpoint is recursive re-expansion of output**, not \"a pass emits zero\n   872\t   new macro datoms\" — per node rather than per sweep, which is what makes\n   873\t   depth a meaningful guard.\n   874\t4. **No phase policy, no `maybe-recompile-at-boundary`.** Both existed to\n   875\t   describe expansion happening inside an executing VM.\n   876\t\n   877\tFrom `cross-language-macro.md`:\n   878\t\n   879\t5. **Frontends do not discover or coerce.** §3 of that document made the\n   880\t   frontend responsible for knowing which names are macros and for\n   881\t   normalizing arguments; both are the expander's, and a call site is an\n   882\t   ordinary application. Its ordering rule (§4: commit definitions first for\n   883\t   durable ids) is retained.\n   884\t\n   885\tFrom the previous revision of this document — the evaluator-integrated\n   886\tdesign, withdrawn 2026-09-13:\n   887\t\n   888\t6. **No runtime expansion inside evaluators.** That design gave the walker\n   889\t   a `:yin/macro-expand` transition, the semantic VM a `:macro-call`\n   890\t   instruction with ephemeral segments and a retention problem, and the\n   891\t   stack VM `OP_MACRO_EXPAND`. All of it was the cost of running a\n   892\t   syntax-side process inside a semantics-side VM. With expansion on its\n   893\t   own medium the machinery has nowhere to live. What runtime expansion\n   894\t   bought — \"the definition arrived after the program loaded\" — is, in a\n   895\t   stream, \"another batch arrived\".\n   896\t7. **No ledger on the VM, no `:macro-eval` / `:macro-authorize` /\n   897\t   `:capability` options, no `:macro-alloc`, no snapshot fields.** The\n   898\t   expander owns its state and its log writer.\n   899\t8. **No macro flags on closures and no \"macro applied as function\" checks**\n   900\t   at three hot-loop sites (its decision 10). Definitions never reach an\n   901\t   evaluator (decision 5), so there is no closure to flag; a missed macro is\n   902\t   an unbound variable.\n   903\t9. **No `:yin/macro-expand` node type** and no frontend `macro-env`\n   904\t   threading through `yang.clojure`, `yang.python`, `yang.php`, or the\n   905\t   shell. In datoms, operands are already syntax.\n   906\t10. **No shadow hints** (`:yang/shadow-params-operand`,\n   907\t    `:yang/shadow-body-start`, and their proposed promotion to facts).\n   908\t    Outermost-first plus lexical shadow tracking in the expander subsumes\n   909\t    them (§3.2).\n   910\t11. **Self-contained output instead of shared subtrees by reference.** That\n   911\t    design's `:existing` emitter option, structural identity rule,\n   912\t    `:keep-eids?` predicate, and forged-eid check served cross-batch\n   913\t    sharing that is only meaningful for durable ids. Copying is the honest\n   914\t    baseline; sharing returns, if at all, as a transactor concern.\n   915\t12. **Events on a log medium, not interleaved with program.** That design\n   916\t    needed `index-datoms` to exclude event entities from root discovery;\n   917\t    here the evaluator never sees one.\n   918\t13. **Kept from that revision:** the explicit `:yin/root` fact (now a codec\n   919\t    improvement on its own merits); the closed-vocabulary validator with\n   920\t    recursive plain-data checks; dual guards and the fixed traversal order;\n   921\t    `mark-tail` as an expander pass; the fuelled body runner; closed syntax\n   922\t    transformers with no store access; `:yin/macro-name` and logical\n   923\t    `:yin/timestamp`; the loader-adapter (never `comp`) correction to the\n   924\t    semantic spec, which stands independently.\n   925\t\n   926\t## Appendix B. Risks the implementer should watch\n   927\t\n   928\t- Land `:yin/root` in the codec before the expander; until then an expanded\n   929\t  batch's root is found by heuristic and copy order matters.\n   930\t- Definitions must be *replaced*, not merely recorded; a forwarded\n   931\t  `(yin/def defn <lambda>)` would give the evaluator a plain function named\n   932\t  `defn` — the exact footgun decision 5 exists to prevent. The Phase 1 final\n   933\t  scan is the guard.\n   934\t- The shadow set must include the params of a lambda in *operator* position\n   935\t  before descending into its body; `let` and `do` lower to exactly that\n   936\t  shape.\n   937\t- `mark-tail` must recurse through `:if` branches and immediately-applied\n   938\t  lambdas; the semantic-VM tail-loop test is the guard.\n   939\t- `run-bounded` must check `:parked` and `:ready-queue` explicitly:\n   940\t  `:vm/park` sets `:halted? true` and `halted?` does not inspect them.\n   941\t- Do not let `:staged` be dropped on `full`; `run-on-stream` relies on\n   942\t  `ready?` staying false until the append succeeds.\n   943\t- yang deletions in §6.2 remove the native `defn` lowering; every test that\n   944\t  used `defn` without `stdlib-forms` now depends on the expander's seeded\n   945\t  store.\n   946\t- Admission must run cycle and dangling-ref checks over the *index*, never\n   947\t  by calling `datoms->ast` first: the decoder is recursive and overflows on\n   948\t  a cycle before any check runs.\n   949\t- `mark-tail` is a recompute over the final tree, not an additive pass over\n   950\t  each expansion's output; an additive pass leaves stale flags on moved\n   951\t  syntax.\n   952\t- Land the `run-on-stream` progress-on-error change before the REPL wires\n   953\t  the expander, or a forwarder-defect throw after a successful batch\n   954\t  duplicates that batch on retry.\n   955\t\n   956\t## Appendix C. Round-2 review findings and their resolution\n   957\t\n   958\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   959\t|---|---|---|\n   960\t| 1 | Dual-medium flush duplicates the program on `out=ok, log=full` (astra P1-2, glm P2-2) | §5 per-medium staging; at-most-once per medium stated; atomicity explicitly not promised |\n   961\t| 2 | Failures never reach the log; deterministic failure head-of-line blocks `program-in` (glm P2-1, astra P2-4) | Decision 11; §3.1 `:error` result carrying the event and `:ctx`; §5 stages only the log, advances, reports `:errors`; §6.1 REPL prints and continues; throw reserved for forwarder defects |\n   962\t| 3 | Later exception replays earlier forwarded batches (astra P1-1) | Accepted as a defect of `run-on-stream`, not of this design; §5 prerequisite and Phase 0 deliverable on `stream-observer`: return progress with the error |\n   963\t| 4 | Operator rewritten into a macro name is not re-expanded (astra P1-3) | §3.2 re-checks the rebuilt node once at the same depth |\n   964\t| 5 | Generated definitions neither installed nor replaced (astra P1-3) | §3.2 rejected in v1 as `:generated-definition`; macro-defining macros reserved |\n   965\t| 6 | Stale store after plain redefinition expands dead code (glm P2-3) | §3.1 harvest is authoritative for every `yin/def`: plain lambda `dissoc`es; §2.2 two-namespaces paragraph; Phase 1 test |\n   966\t| 7 | Alloc not monotonic; gensyms repeat across batches (astra P2-5, glm P3-4) | §3.1 seed includes `(:next-eid alloc)`; retry reuses staged allocation |\n   967\t| 8 | Final scan must be scope-aware (astra P2-6, glm P3-8) | §3.1 step 8 scans only for `:macro?` lambdas; application fixpoint is by construction via #4 |\n   968\t| 9 | Tail marking must clear stale flags; lambda rule ambiguous (astra P2-7) | §3.4 whole-tree recompute with per-node context rules; lambda body always tail; `linearize` owns inlining |\n   969\t| 10 | Provenance identity: same tempid in two batches, generated syntax, program↔log correspondence, event schema home, anonymous macros (astra P2-8) | §4.1 `:yin/source-batch`; nested `source-call` into the log copy; `expansion-root` names the log copy, correspondence not promised; `macro/event-schema`; `:yin/macro-name` absent for inline lambdas |\n   970\t| 11 | Output validation alone does not close the sandbox input (astra P2-9) | §3.1 step 1 admission validates ground values; §3.3 validates operands going in; `:eval`/prelude trust contract stated; fuel and payload limits recorded as known v1 limits |\n   971\t| 12 | Cycle detection after recursive decode overflows; `eid(node)` undefined on maps (astra P2-10, glm P3-8) | §3.1 admission over the index; working-representation paragraph; Appendix B |\n   972\t| 13 | \"As in Clojure\" is wrong for syntactic harvest; \"helpers are other macros\" unsupported; macro/runtime namespace relation unstated (astra P3-11) | §3.1 policy stated on its own terms; §3.3 helpers are prelude only, body expansion reserved; §2.2 two namespaces |\n   973\t| 14 | `program-out` `m = ev` would be a cross-medium ref; event attrs dropped from schema but still emitted (glm P3-5) | Decision 9 and §4.1: `program-out` carries `default-op`; `macro/event-schema` |\n   974\t| 15 | Root fact unconditional vs \"compile output unchanged\" (glm P3-6) | Phase 0 test reworded \"modulo the root fact\" |\n   975\t| 16 | Phase 2 `:semantic` test has an unstated prerequisite (glm P3-7) | Phase 2 defers it to the semantic spec's Phases 1–2, with the continuation-depth measurement |\n   976\t| 17 | Missed-macro diagnostics (glm P3-9) | §6.1 `repl-state` lists the store's names |\n   977\t| — | Qualify \"never holds VM control state\" (astra, layering) | §1.2 table: the *program* evaluator's state |\n   978\t| — | Later batch ≠ expansion inside a loaded continuation (astra P3-11) | Appendix A #6 already states the limit; decision 11 makes the consumed-batch semantics explicit |\n   979\t\n   980\tRound 3 (`collab/1789279450960-review-macro-stream-process-r3.{gpt-6-astra,glm-5.3}.findings.md`), on revision 3:\n   981\t\n   982\t| # | Finding (reviewer) | Resolution |\n   983\t|---|---|---|\n   984\t| 18 | Re-check happens after operands expanded; a newly discovered binder macro gets rewritten operands (astra P1-1) | §3.2 application branch: operator first, re-check, then operands; test with `((choose) f [x] (x 1))` |\n   985\t| 19 | `(defn m …)` after `(defmacro m …)` not caught — the `yin/def` shape appears only post-expansion; non-lambda redefinitions ignored (astra P2-2) | §3.1 step 2 `dissoc`es on any non-macro value; new step 5 post-harvest over the final tree, effective next batch; ordering rule stated |\n   986\t| 20 | Inline generated macro lambda both required by a test and forbidden by the validator (astra P2-3) | Prohibition kept and widened to `:generated-macro`; the test case removed; both reserved together |\n   987\t| 21 | `:errors` never drained; a stale error suppresses later evaluation; A-ok/B-error in one call skips A (astra P2-4, glm P2-1) | §5 `drain-errors` read-and-reset with `:forwarded`; §6.1 drives the evaluator on `:forwarded > 0` independently of `:errors` |\n   988\t| 22 | Admission checks only root-reachable, harvest decodes the whole batch (astra P2-5) | §3.1 step 1 checks every entity in the index |\n   989\t| 23 | Admission failures have no event path (astra P2-6) | §4.1 admission-failure event allocated from the watermark, no input values in diagnostics |\n   990\t| 24 | `run-on-stream` error shape: a value-shaped error lets un-updated callers continue silently (glm P3-2); load vs flush failure must carry different cursors (astra) | §5 prerequisite: keep the throw, carry `:session` in `ex-data`, cursor position depends on where the failure was; Phase 0 tests |\n   991\t| 25 | Admission should check the closed node vocabulary (glm P3-3) | §3.1 step 1 `:unknown-type` |\n   992\t| 26 | `closed`/`invalid-value`/`transport-error` are protocol outcomes, not defects; `full` may be permanent (astra) | §5 table reworded; outcome and both slots preserved in the throw |\n   993\t| 27 | Decision 5 \"unbound variable\" too strong given §2.2; decisions 10/11 out of order; §3.4 exactness under the no-inline contract (astra, glm) | Decision 5 qualified; order fixed; §3.4 sentence added |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
